{
  "platform": "\n    <figure class=\"platform-diagram\" aria-label=\"Conceptual relationship: JDK includes development tools and the JRE runtime; JRE includes standard libraries and the JVM.\">\n      <figcaption><span>THE JAVA PLATFORM</span><span class=\"platform-equation\">JDK <i>\u2283</i> JRE <i>\u2283</i> JVM</span></figcaption>\n      <div class=\"platform-layer platform-jdk\">\n        <div class=\"platform-heading\"><strong>JDK</strong><span>Java Development Kit<small>Develop Java programs</small></span></div>\n        <div class=\"platform-tools\"><span>Development tools</span><code>javac</code><code>javadoc</code><code>jdb</code><code>jar</code></div>\n        <div class=\"platform-layer platform-jre\">\n          <div class=\"platform-heading\"><strong>JRE</strong><span>Java Runtime Environment<small>Run Java programs</small></span></div>\n          <div class=\"platform-libraries\"><b>Standard libraries</b><span>java.lang \u00b7 java.util \u00b7 java.io \u00b7 java.net</span></div>\n          <div class=\"platform-layer platform-jvm\">\n            <div class=\"platform-heading\"><strong>JVM</strong><span>Java Virtual Machine<small>Load and execute bytecode</small></span></div>\n            <div class=\"platform-parts\"><span>Class loading</span><span>Interpreter / JIT</span><span>Memory &amp; GC</span></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"platform-footnote\">Conceptual view \u00b7 Runtime components are included in the JDK; a separate JRE package is not required.</p>\n    </figure>",
  "skeleton": "\n    <figure class=\"skeleton-figure\">\n      <figcaption><span class=\"visual-type\">01 / Source code</span><strong>Write this in your editor</strong><span class=\"skeleton-file\">First.java</span></figcaption>\n      <div class=\"skeleton-source\"><pre><code><span class=\"sk-mark\">\u2460</span> <span class=\"sk-key\">public class</span> <span class=\"sk-name\">First</span> {\n<span class=\"sk-mark\">\u2461</span>     <span class=\"sk-key\">public static void</span> <span class=\"sk-name\">main</span>(String[] args) {\n<span class=\"sk-mark\">\u2462</span>         System.out.println(<span class=\"sk-string\">\"Hello World\"</span>);\n          }\n      }</code></pre></div>\n      <div class=\"skeleton-explanations\">\n        <div><b>\u2460 Class \u00b7 the outer container</b><p><code>public class First</code> declares a public class named <code>First</code>. Its name matches <code>First.java</code>.</p></div>\n        <div><b>\u2461 Method \u00b7 inside the class</b><p><code>main</code> is the entry point used here. Its braces contain the instructions to execute.</p></div>\n        <div><b>\u2462 Statement \u00b7 inside the method</b><p><code>System</code> is a class; <code>out</code> is its standard-output stream; <code>println</code> prints the string and a newline. The semicolon ends the statement.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"skeleton-nesting\"><span>Class <code>{ }</code></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u2283</span><span>Method <code>{ }</code></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u2283</span><span>Statement <code>;</code></span></div>\n    </figure>\n    <div class=\"skeleton-signature\">\n      <h4>Read the main method, one piece at a time</h4>\n      <dl>\n        <div><dt>public</dt><dd>Access modifier: the method is accessible from outside the class.</dd></div>\n        <div><dt>static</dt><dd>No First object is needed to invoke this method.</dd></div>\n        <div><dt>void</dt><dd>Return type: this method returns no value.</dd></div>\n        <div><dt>main</dt><dd>The method name recognized by the launcher for this entry-point form.</dd></div>\n        <div><dt>String[] args</dt><dd>Parameter: an array of command-line strings. args is the chosen variable name.</dd></div>\n      </dl>\n    </div>\n    <figure class=\"skeleton-figure skeleton-terminal\">\n      <figcaption><span class=\"visual-type\">02 / Command line</span><strong>Run these in a terminal</strong></figcaption>\n      <p class=\"skeleton-context\">Save First.java, then open a terminal in that folder with a JDK configured. Enter each command separately.</p>\n      <ol class=\"skeleton-run\">\n        <li><div><span class=\"skeleton-action\">COMPILE</span><code>javac First.java</code></div><p>The JDK compiler reads the source and creates <strong>First.class</strong> (bytecode). Successful compilation normally prints nothing.</p></li>\n        <li><div><span class=\"skeleton-action\">RUN</span><code>java -cp . First</code></div><p>The launcher starts a JVM and invokes <strong>First.main</strong>. <code>-cp .</code> searches the current folder; <code>First</code> is the class name, without an extension.</p></li>\n      </ol>\n      <div class=\"skeleton-output\"><span>PROGRAM OUTPUT</span><samp>Hello World</samp></div>\n      <div class=\"skeleton-path\"><span>First.java<small>Source code</small></span><b aria-hidden=\"true\">\u2192</b><span>First.class<small>Compiled bytecode</small></span><b aria-hidden=\"true\">\u2192</b><span>JVM \u2192 main<small>Program execution</small></span></div>\n      <p class=\"skeleton-context\">With the default classpath, <code>java First</code> also works. These are terminal commands; they do not go inside the Java file.</p>\n    </figure>\n    <aside class=\"skeleton-args\"><strong>Where do args come from?</strong><p><code>java -cp . First Candice 2026</code></p><p><code>args[0] = \"Candice\"</code> \u00b7 <code>args[1] = \"2026\"</code></p><p>Both values are strings. This example does not read args, so its output remains <code>Hello World</code>.</p></aside>"
}